  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Monday...\n\nPlease be aware that it is going to be very HOT this weekend. \nPlan your outdoor activities accordingly!\n\nMorning showers and t-storms continue (especially in the\nnortheast) w/ a shortwave passage and healthy early morning\nsoutheasterly low level jet. Instability is modest so not\nexpecting severe wx, but anomalously high pwats (1.30\" east of \nBillings per mesoanalysis) will yield areas of heavy rain. By\nafternoon, with another weak shortwave and diurnal heating, expect\nscattered thunderstorms into the evening. Shear and instability \nsupport a risk of a few severe storms capable of producing large \nhail, strong winds and heavy rain. \n\nLook for scattered diurnal thunderstorms again on Wednesday,\nthough the air mass will begin to turn drier in the zonal flow.\nA few strong to severe storms are possible, with hail, wind and\nlocally heavy rain being the main risks. Thursday's air mass will\nbe drier yet and this is looking like a mostly dry day, but a few\ndiurnal showers/t-storms are possible in the east (depending on\nthe timing of a final weak shortwave, of which there is model\nuncertainty). Temperatures today through Thursday will be\nseasonable. Look for highs in the 80s to lower 90s most places.\n\nHeights build Friday in response to a digging trof off the\nPacific coast. This will be a warmer day (temps in 90s) and should\nbe mostly dry, but a PV max ejecting out of the northern great\nbasin may bring isolated weak diurnal convection over the\nsouthern mountains and perhaps far southeast MT. This is highly \nuncertain and as of now the forecast for Friday is dry.\n\nConfidence is high for an anomalously strong upper level high over\nthe intermountain west this weekend. This is guaranteed to be the\nhottest air mass yet this summer. Triple digit highs are likely,\nand this coupled with increasingly warmer nighttime temps offering\nminimal relief, will result in heat related impacts Saturday and \nSunday, and possibly Monday. Make sure to take precautions to \nprotect yourself from the heat.\n\nThe upcoming hot spell may not just be run-of-the-mill July heat.\nDeterministic models continue to show 700mb temps to +20C by\nSunday. With typical mid-summer mixing (lofted smoke from the\nsouth could be a player here), this would yield high temps quite a\nbit above 100F (on the order of 106F at Billings and 108F at \nMiles City). A little downslope wind could push temps even hotter.\nIt is not inconceivable that we threaten all-time record temps in\nour cwa by Sunday (see table below).\n\nEnsemble consensus is for a bit of cooling to be introduced by\nMonday, and eventually an increase in monsoon moisture by Monday \nor Tuesday. Even with slight cooling there is high confidence that\ntemps will remain well above normal thru at least the first part \nof next week.\n\nHere are all-time records at each of our main climate stations:\nBillings   108F\nLivingston 105F\nMiles City 111F\nSheridan   107F\nBaker      109F\n\nJKL",
